<!-- SECTION 1 -->
<h2>Quick Answer — Male and Female Averages at 15</h2>
<div class="quick-grid">
<div class="quick-card">
<div class="quick-card-label">15-Year-Old Male — Average</div>
<div class="quick-card-val">5.4–5.8s</div>
<div class="quick-card-sub">General population, no specific sprint training</div>
</div>
<div class="quick-card">
<div class="quick-card-label">15-Year-Old Female — Average</div>
<div class="quick-card-val">6.0–6.6s</div>
<div class="quick-card-sub">General population, no specific sprint training</div>
</div>
</div>
<p>These ranges represent 15-year-olds with normal athletic activity but no dedicated speed training. JV and freshman-level athletes who play speed-dependent sports typically run 0.3 to 0.5 seconds faster than these population averages.</p>
<p>At 15, the 40-yard dash is primarily a development baseline rather than a recruiting metric. <strong>This is actually the best possible time to test</strong> — you have three full years of natural development and training ahead, which means improvements you make now compound across your entire high school career.</p>
