<!-- SECTION 4 -->
<h2>Why 15 Is the Best Age to Start Speed Training</h2>
<p>The development window from 15 to 18 is the most productive period for speed improvement in a male athlete's life. Natural hormonal development drives gains independent of training. The body responds more rapidly to speed training stimuli than at any other age. And there is enough time remaining to build a real foundation rather than rushing technical fixes in the weeks before a senior combine.</p>
<h3>What a 15-Year-Old Should Focus On Instead of Their Time</h3>
<p>At 15, the most valuable thing you can do with your 40-yard dash is establish a baseline and identify your weaknesses. Have a coach watch your start: a poor 3-point stance or slow first-step drive accounts for 0.1 to 0.2 seconds of slowness before you take three steps. Most 15-year-olds have never been coached on stance mechanics — which means that fix alone produces measurable improvement within days, not weeks.</p>
<h3>The 15-to-18 Natural Speed Development Window</h3>
<p>For most males at 15, you are at the beginning of the steepest natural development curve in your athletic life. Testosterone levels typically accelerate between 15 and 17, driving fast-twitch muscle fiber development and power production increases that are independent of any training you do. A 15-year-old male who does nothing specific for speed will typically run 0.2 to 0.4 seconds faster at 18 simply from natural growth. Adding deliberate speed and strength training on top of this natural development compounds the gains dramatically.</p>
