<h3>The Three Training Moves That Drop a 16-Year-Old's Time Fastest</h3>
<ul>
<li><strong>Fix your stance and first step (0.1–0.2s in days).</strong> Most 16-year-olds have never been coached on 40-yard dash technique. A proper 3-point stance, 45-degree forward lean, and explosive first-step drive off the back foot can drop your time noticeably without any fitness changes.</li>
<li><strong>Sled pushes (0.15–0.3s in 6–8 weeks).</strong> Resisted sprinting with 10 to 20 percent of your bodyweight is the most efficient way to build first-step acceleration at any age. Two sessions per week of 5-to-6 twenty-yard sled pushes produces significant improvements over one off-season.</li>
<li><strong>Squat to 1.5× bodyweight (0.1–0.2s over one season).</strong> Ground force production is the engine of the 40-yard dash. Athletes who squat less than 1.0× bodyweight consistently underperform in the first 10 yards. Building strength is the most durable speed investment you can make at 16. Use our <a href="https://athletecalculator.com/squat-strength-calculator">Squat Strength Predictor</a> to see what your current squat predicts.</li>
</ul>
